 *  4. Then the cart cleanup, in the monolith's own shape: `books.map(...)` fires one
 *     DeleteItem per element and `await Promise.all(...)` waits for all of them
 *     (`#business_rules[4]`, perf hint PH-1 -- it is not a sequential loop). A duplicate
 *     bookId really does delete the same key twice (`#business_rules[9]`); a bookId that
 *     is not in the cart is a successful no-op (`#business_rules[3]`); only these bookIds
 *     go, the rest of the cart stays (case `order-clears-only-ordered-items`).
 *     If any delete fails -- e.g. an element without `bookId`, whose key is incomplete
 *     (`#business_rules[10]`) -- the request answers 500 `{error, stack}` with the order
 *     row already written and the cart partly cleared (`#business_rules[5]`).
 *     NO transaction, NO rollback: SI-15 / D-19 say the monolith's non-atomic behaviour is
 *     the contract, and "fixing" it would be an unauthorised deviation.
 *  5. Where the monolith called `updateBestSellers(books)` without awaiting it
 *     (`routes/orders.js:87`, return value unused -- implementation-trace SI-8/SI-9), the
 *     target sends ONE SQS message to BestsellerUpdateQueue, awaited before returning
 *     (D-3 / D-36: a Lambda that has returned has its environment frozen, an un-awaited
 *     send would be lost). Body `{orderId, books:[{bookId, quantity}]}`, with `orderId` as
 *     the idempotency key and one element per ordered book -- duplicates are NOT merged
 *     here, the consumer merges them (D-3). No bestseller code runs in this function (D-2).
 *     A send failure is logged only: the order response never depends on the update
 *     (`#business_rules[7]`, `#responses[1]`, SI-8 / D-36).
 *  6. 200 `{message:'Order created successfully', orderId}` -- no totals, no item count
 *     (`#responses[1]`).
 *
 * Identity: only `claims.sub` (D-5 / D-10); `x-customer-id` and any body `customerId` are
